messages:
Natasha L.: Quick sync — running into a weird one: when we start a streaming completion (okhttp + our auth interceptor) the Authorization header sometimes disappears mid-stream and the server rejects the socket. Repro steps below, any ideas?

Ben S.: Uh oh. Are you adding the header in an application interceptor or network interceptor? Also, is this only when using the shadowJar artifact?

Natasha L.: application interceptor. It happens on our shaded client artifact (shadowJar + relocate). Non-shaded local build seems fine. Minimal interceptor:```java
class AuthInterceptor implements Interceptor {
  @Override public Response intercept(Chain chain) throws IOException {
    Request req = chain.request().newBuilder()
      .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + tokenProvider.get())
      .build();
    return chain.proceed(req);
  }
}
```

Ravi P.: Two quick angles: 1) OkHttp has subtle behavior with interceptors + streaming bodies (if you mutate the request after the stream starts you can lose headers). 2) shading can break ServiceLoader-based plumbing or relocations for okhttp/jackson/gson which changes runtime wiring.

Ben S.: Yep. Also check if anything is replacing the call with a different client type at runtime (relocated packages sometimes leave duplicates). Do you have a stacktrace or a captured raw request from the server side?

Natasha L.: Server logs show initial handshake with Authorization present then subsequent frames without it (403 after a few tokens). I captured client logs: shows header present on initial Request, then later in the same connection it sends a follow-up request (http/1.1 chunked) without Authorization.

Ravi P.: That sounds like a proxy/redirect or an internal re-issue of the request (okhttp may re-create a follow-up for redirects/auth challenges). If the follow-up is created by OkHttp it will only copy selective headers unless you set them on the new request. Application interceptors run before redirects follow-ups; network interceptors see the follow-up traffic. If you need the header always carried across follow-ups, add it in a network interceptor OR set it via authenticating with an Authenticator that injects header into follow-ups.

Ben S.: Example fix (network interceptor):```java
client = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
  .addNetworkInterceptor(chain -> {
    Request r = chain.request().newBuilder()
      .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + tokenProvider.get())
      .build();
    return chain.proceed(r);
  })
  .build();
```This ensures header is present on follow-ups created by the transport. Might be fine as a short-term patch.

Natasha L.: Good call. But why does it only fail with the shaded artifact? Local non-shaded works.

Ravi P.: My money is on shading relocating Jackson/Gson or okhttp resources so some of the internal classes or ServiceLoader entries are duplicated/mismatched. For example, if shadowJar relocates com.squareup.okhttp3 but some libs still reference okhttp3 directly via reflection, you can end up with two class versions and runtime chooses the wrong one. Also check the shadowJar merge strategy for META-INF/services/* — those can be lost.

Ben S.: We had a separate bug where our shadow relocation also bundled an old version of okhttp's internal platform code which caused different follow-up behavior. Quick checks: run with -verbose:class to see which okhttp3 classes are loaded and from which jar. Also grep your shaded artifact for duplicate okhttp classes.

Natasha L.: Got it. I inspected the jar and found relocated okhttp3 classes plus an extra copy under original packages (ugh). That explains mismatched behavior.

Jordan K.: Quick note on serialization side — we also saw Jackson/Gson collisions when shading both libraries. If you relocate both, keep only the one you actually use at runtime or relocate with distinct prefixes and ensure Resource merging for Jackson modules (META-INF/services/com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.Module). Otherwise you can silently fall back to Gson for some paths which changes content-negotiation and headers.

Natasha L.: Action items I can do now: 1) switch to addNetworkInterceptor to keep Authorization on follow-ups, 2) fix shadow rules to avoid duplicate okhttp classes and merge META-INF/services, 3) rebuild shaded client and run integration. Anyone object?

Ben S.: +1 on all three. Also add a unit/integration test that simulates a redirect/follow-up and asserts header preserved. We had no coverage for follow-ups.

Ravi P.: For shadow config (Gradle shadow plugin) we used something like:```gradle
shadowJar {
  relocate 'com.squareup.okhttp3', 'shade.okhttp3'
  // exclude okhttp internals that are bundled by other deps
  dependencies { exclude(dependency(':some-old-okhttp:')) }
  mergeServiceFiles()
  transform(com.github.jengelman.gradle.plugins.shadow.transformers.AppendingTransformer) {
    resource = 'META-INF/services/com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.Module'
  }
}
```Make sure you do NOT accidentally produce two copies under original and relocated packages.

